      "text": "In typical counterclockwise atrial flutter, inferior-to-superior activation of the right atrial septum produces negative inferior-lead waves with a slow downward and rapid upward slope and positive V1 waves with craniocaudal lateral-wall activation, without an isoelectric line; these F waves have a cycle length of 250–170 milliseconds (240–350 beats per minute), whereas clockwise flutter reverses the pattern to positive inferior-lead and negative V1 waves; both patterns are sawtooth, especially in DII, DIII and aVF, and may be altered after surgery, catheter ablation or antiarrhythmic treatment.",
      "population": "",
      "numbers": "",
      "source": "Article: Abdala-Lizarraga J, Quesada-Ocete J, Quesada-Ocete B et al. Cavotricuspid Isthmus-Dependent Atrial Flutter. Beyond Simple Linear Ablation. Reviews in Cardiovascular Medicine 2024",
